// EXPECT: E0256 — a `#foreign` procedure used as a value (ADR-0059 §5)
//
// Taking a procedure as a value — `f := add` — is how a procedure pointer is made (ADR-0059 §1). A
// `#foreign` procedure's type is `ContextKind::CCall` and the VM reaches it through libffi rather
// than a `ProcRef`, so an indirect call to one is a *second* calling convention this wave does not
// build. It is refused at the point the value is taken.
//
// **Refused, not a type mismatch.** `puts` does have a procedure type, so `g := puts` would
// otherwise type fine and fail only at the eventual indirect call — or worse, lower to a `ProcValue`
// the VM's `ProcRef` path could not resolve. E0256 catches it where the reader wrote it, and its own
// code lets the message say "yet" rather than reporting a type error a reader cannot act on.
//
// A **direct** call to a `#foreign` procedure is still legal — that is the whole point of `#foreign`
// — so the objection is specifically to taking one as a value. The callee of a call routes through a
// path that does not refuse; only value position does.

libc :: #system_library "c";

/// A `#foreign` procedure declared in *this* file, so its use resolves to a same-file item and
/// reaches the E0256 check rather than the cross-file refusal (ADR-0059 §1) an imported one would.
puts :: (s: *u8) -> s64 #foreign libc "puts";

grab_foreign :: () {
    // The refusal: a `#foreign` procedure taken as a value.
    g := puts;
}
